# Coding Standards & Style Guide

> **How to use this file:**
> This is a template showing comprehensive coding standards. Customize to match your actual standards.
> Rename to `coding-standards.md` when ready to use.

## Overview

This document defines the coding standards for all projects in this workspace. These standards ensure consistency, maintainability, and quality across the codebase.

## Language-Specific Guidelines

### TypeScript

#### Type Safety
```typescript
// ✅ Good - explicit types, no any
function processUser(userId: string, name: string): Promise<User> {
  // implementation
}

// ❌ Bad - any types, missing return type
function processUser(userId: any, name: any): any {
  // implementation
}

// ✅ Good - use interfaces for clarity
interface CreateUserRequest {
  email: string;
  name: string;
  role: 'admin' | 'user' | 'guest';
}

// ❌ Bad - loose object types
function createUser(user: Record<string, any>): void {
  // implementation
}
```

#### Strict Mode
- Always use `"strict": true` in tsconfig.json
- No `// @ts-ignore` comments without justification
- Enable `"noImplicitAny"` and `"strictNullChecks"`

#### Generics
```typescript
// ✅ Good - generic utility functions
function getCacheItem<T>(key: string): T | null {
  // implementation
}

// ✅ Good - constrained generics
function merge<T extends object>(a: T, b: T): T {
  return { ...a, ...b };
}

// ❌ Bad - overly broad generics
function process<T>(input: T): T {
  // implementation
}
```

### JavaScript/Node.js

#### Async/Await
```javascript
// ✅ Good - async/await is more readable
async function fetchUserData(userId) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('HTTP error: ' + response.status);
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    logger.error('Failed to fetch user', { userId, error });
    throw error;
  }
}

// ❌ Bad - promise chains are harder to read
function fetchUserData(userId) {
  return fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .catch(error => logger.error('Failed', { userId, error }));
}
```

#### Error Handling
```javascript
// ✅ Good - specific error types
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message, field) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
    this.field = field;
  }
}

try {
  validateEmail(email);
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof ValidationError) {
    // Handle validation error specifically
  } else {
    // Handle unexpected errors
  }
}

// ❌ Bad - generic error handling
try {
  validateEmail(email);
} catch (e) {
  console.log('Error: ' + e);
}
```

### React/JSX

#### Component Structure
```typescript
// ✅ Good - clear functional component structure
interface UserCardProps {
  userId: string;
  onSelect?: (userId: string) => void;
  isLoading?: boolean;
}

export const UserCard: React.FC<UserCardProps> = ({
  userId,
  onSelect,
  isLoading = false,
}) => {
  const [user, setUser] = React.useState<User | null>(null);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    loadUser(userId).then(setUser);
  }, [userId]);

  if (isLoading || !user) return <Skeleton />;

  return (
    <div className="user-card">
      <h3>{user.name}</h3>
      <button onClick={() => onSelect?.(userId)}>Select</button>
    </div>
  );
};

// ❌ Bad - no types, unclear structure
function UserCard(props) {
  const [user, setUser] = React.useState();
  React.useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${props.userId}`).then(r => r.json()).then(setUser);
  }, [props.userId]);
  return <div>{user && <h3>{user.name}</h3>}</div>;
}
```

#### Hooks
```typescript
// ✅ Good - custom hooks for logic
function useUserData(userId: string) {
  const [user, setUser] = React.useState<User | null>(null);
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = React.useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = React.useState<Error | null>(null);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    setIsLoading(true);

    fetchUser(userId)
      .then(user => !cancelled && setUser(user))
      .catch(err => !cancelled && setError(err))
      .finally(() => !cancelled && setIsLoading(false));

    return () => { cancelled = true; };
  }, [userId]);

  return { user, isLoading, error };
}

// ✅ Good - dependency arrays are explicit
React.useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setTimeout(() => setVisible(false), 5000);
  return () => clearTimeout(timer);
}, [setVisible]); // All dependencies listed

// ❌ Bad - missing dependency
React.useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
}, []); // Missing 'count' dependency!
```

## File Organization

### Directory Structure
```
src/
├── components/          # Reusable React components
│   ├── UserCard/
│   │   ├── UserCard.tsx
│   │   ├── UserCard.test.tsx
│   │   └── index.ts
│   └── Layout/
├── hooks/              # Custom React hooks
│   ├── useUserData.ts
│   └── useAuth.ts
├── services/           # API clients, external integrations
│   ├── userService.ts
│   └── authService.ts
├── utils/              # Pure utility functions
│   ├── validators.ts
│   ├── formatters.ts
│   └── date-utils.ts
├── types/              # TypeScript type definitions
│   ├── user.types.ts
│   └── api.types.ts
├── App.tsx            # Root component
└── index.ts           # Entry point
```

### File Naming
- Components: PascalCase, `UserCard.tsx`
- Services: camelCase, `userService.ts`
- Utilities: kebab-case, `string-utils.ts`
- Types: PascalCase + .types extension, `User.types.ts`
- Tests: Same name + `.test.ts`, `UserCard.test.tsx`

## Code Formatting

### Indentation
- Use 2 spaces for indentation (not tabs)
- Continuation lines indented 4 spaces
- No trailing whitespace

### Line Length
- Maximum 100 characters
- Break long lines at logical points

### Spacing
```typescript
// ✅ Good spacing
function calculate(a: number, b: number): number {
  const result = a + b;
  return result * 2;
}

// No space before parentheses in function declarations
function calculate(a: number) {}

// Space before { in control structures
if (condition) {
  // body
}

// Space after commas
const items = [1, 2, 3];
```

## Comments

### When to Comment
- Explain the WHY, not the WHAT
- Document non-obvious algorithms
- Explain trade-offs and decisions
- Add warnings for gotchas

### Comment Format
```typescript
// ✅ Good - explains intent
// Retry with exponential backoff to avoid overwhelming the service
async function retryWithBackoff<T>(
  fn: () => Promise<T>,
  maxAttempts: number = 3
): Promise<T> {
  // implementation
}

// ✅ Good - documents assumptions
// WARNING: This assumes the data is sorted by date.
// The date field MUST be set before calling this function.
function filterRecentItems(items: Item[]): Item[] {
  // implementation
}

// ❌ Bad - just restates code
const count = 0; // Set count to 0

// ❌ Bad - outdated comment
// TODO: Fix this later (written 2 years ago)
```

### JSDoc Comments
```typescript
/**
 * Validates an email address format.
 *
 * Checks for valid email format but does NOT verify the address exists.
 * For real validation, send a verification email.
 *
 * @param email - Email address to validate
 * @returns true if format is valid, false otherwise
 *
 * @example
 * isValidEmail('user@example.com'); // true
 * isValidEmail('invalid-email');    // false
 *
 * @throws Will throw if email is not a string
 */
function isValidEmail(email: string): boolean {
  // implementation
}
```

## Testing Standards

### Test Structure
```typescript
describe('UserService', () => {
  describe('getUser', () => {
    it('should return user by id', async () => {
      // Arrange
      const userId = 'user-123';
      const expectedUser = { id: userId, name: 'John' };
      mock.db.query.mockResolvedValue(expectedUser);

      // Act
      const result = await UserService.getUser(userId);

      // Assert
      expect(result).toEqual(expectedUser);
    });

    it('should throw error if user not found', async () => {
      // Arrange
      mock.db.query.mockResolvedValue(null);

      // Act & Assert
      await expect(UserService.getUser('invalid-id'))
        .rejects
        .toThrow('User not found');
    });
  });
});
```

### Test Naming
- Test names should describe the scenario and expected result
- Format: `should [expected behavior] [when context]`
- Examples:
  - `should return user by id`
  - `should throw error when user not found`
  - `should handle network timeout gracefully`

### Coverage Targets
- Minimum 85% code coverage
- 100% coverage for critical paths (auth, payments, data access)
- High-value tests > high code coverage (test behavior, not lines)

## Imports and Exports

### Import Order
```typescript
// 1. External libraries
import React from 'react';
import { useState } from 'react';

// 2. Node.js built-ins
import { readFile } from 'fs/promises';

// 3. Internal absolute imports
import { UserService } from '@/services/UserService';

// 4. Internal relative imports
import { formatDate } from '../utils/formatDate';

// 5. Type imports last
import type { User } from '@/types/User.types';
```

### Named Exports (Preferred)
```typescript
// ✅ Good - named exports
export function validateEmail(email: string): boolean {
  // implementation
}

export function formatEmail(email: string): string {
  // implementation
}

// Usage: import { validateEmail, formatEmail } from '@/utils/email';
```

### Default Exports (Components Only)
```typescript
// ✅ Good - default export for components
export default function UserCard() {
  // implementation
}

// Usage: import UserCard from '@/components/UserCard';

// ❌ Bad - default export for utilities
export default function validateEmail() {
  // implementation
}
```

## Naming Conventions

### Variables and Constants
```typescript
// ✅ Good - descriptive names
const MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS = 3;
const isLoading = true;
const userIds = ['id1', 'id2'];
const defaultTimeout = 5000;

// ❌ Bad - unclear names
const m = 3;
const x = true;
const arr = ['id1', 'id2'];
const t = 5000;

// ✅ Good - boolean prefix
const isVisible = true;
const hasError = false;
const shouldRetry = true;
const canDelete = false;

// ❌ Bad - no boolean indicator
const visible = true;
const error = false;
const retry = true;
const delete_ = false;
```

### Functions and Methods
```typescript
// ✅ Good - verb + noun
function calculateTotal(): number {}
function validateEmail(): boolean {}
function formatDate(): string {}
function getUserById(): User {}

// ❌ Bad - noun only or vague
function total(): number {}
function email(): boolean {}
function format(): string {}
function get(): User {}
```

## Logging

### Log Levels
```typescript
// DEBUG - Detailed diagnostic information
logger.debug('Cache hit for key', { key, duration: 12 });

// INFO - General informational messages
logger.info('User logged in', { userId, timestamp });

// WARN - Warning conditions, potential issues
logger.warn('Database query slow', { query: 'SELECT...', duration: 5000 });

// ERROR - Error conditions
logger.error('Failed to process payment', { orderId, error });

// FATAL - System is unusable
logger.fatal('Database connection lost, shutting down');
```

### Logging Best Practices
```typescript
// ✅ Good - context included
logger.error('Failed to fetch user', {
  userId,
  attemptNumber: 3,
  error: error.message,
  errorCode: error.code,
});

// ❌ Bad - minimal context
logger.error('Error: ' + error);

// ✅ Good - structured logging
logger.info('User registered', {
  userId,
  email: user.email,
  timestamp: new Date(),
  source: 'api',
});

// ❌ Bad - unstructured
logger.info(`User ${user.email} registered at ${new Date()}`);
```

## Configuration

### Environment Variables
```
# ✅ Good - clear naming convention
APP_NAME=CloudDash
APP_ENV=production
DB_HOST=postgres.example.com
DB_PORT=5432
DB_NAME=cloudash
REDIS_HOST=redis.example.com
API_KEY_PROVIDER=some-provider
FEATURE_FLAG_NEW_DASHBOARD=true
```

### Runtime Config
```typescript
// ✅ Good - validated at startup
interface Config {
  port: number;
  dbUrl: string;
  logLevel: 'debug' | 'info' | 'warn' | 'error';
}

function loadConfig(): Config {
  const port = process.env.PORT ? parseInt(process.env.PORT) : 3000;
  const dbUrl = process.env.DB_URL;
  if (!dbUrl) throw new Error('DB_URL environment variable required');

  return { port, dbUrl, logLevel: 'info' };
}

// ❌ Bad - hardcoded values, missing validation
const port = 3000;
const dbUrl = 'localhost';
```

## Performance Guidelines

### Avoid Common Pitfalls
```typescript
// ❌ Bad - N+1 query problem
async function getUsersWithPosts(userIds: string[]) {
  const users = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id IN (...)', userIds);
  // This loop causes N additional queries!
  const result = users.map(async (user) => ({
    ...user,
    posts: await db.query('SELECT * FROM posts WHERE user_id = ?', user.id),
  }));
  return Promise.all(result);
}

// ✅ Good - batch load with join
async function getUsersWithPosts(userIds: string[]) {
  return db.query(`
    SELECT u.*, json_agg(p.*) as posts
    FROM users u
    LEFT JOIN posts p ON u.id = p.user_id
    WHERE u.id IN (...)
    GROUP BY u.id
  `, userIds);
}
```

### Memoization
```typescript
// ✅ Good - cache expensive computations
const expensiveCalculation = memoize((input: string) => {
  // Heavy computation here
  return result;
});

// ✅ Good - React.memo for expensive components
export const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent(props: Props) {
  return <div>{/* complex rendering */}</div>;
}, (prevProps, nextProps) => {
  // Return true if props are equal (no re-render needed)
  return prevProps.id === nextProps.id;
});
```

## Security Guidelines

### Input Validation
```typescript
// ✅ Good - validate all inputs
function createUser(email: string, password: string): void {
  if (!isValidEmail(email)) throw new Error('Invalid email');
  if (password.length < 12) throw new Error('Password too short');
  // create user
}

// ❌ Bad - trust user input
function createUser(email: string, password: string): void {
  // Just use what they gave us
}
```

### SQL Injection Prevention
```typescript
// ✅ Good - parameterized queries
const user = await db.query(
  'SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1 AND id = $2',
  [email, userId]
);

// ❌ Bad - string concatenation
const user = await db.query(
  `SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '${email}' AND id = ${userId}`
);
```

### Secrets Management
```typescript
// ✅ Good - environment variables
const apiKey = process.env.EXTERNAL_API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) throw new Error('EXTERNAL_API_KEY not set');

// ❌ Bad - hardcoded secrets
const apiKey = 'sk-12345...';

// ❌ Bad - in version control
const config = {
  apiKey: 'actual-secret',
  token: 'another-secret',
};
```

## Deprecated Patterns

These patterns should NOT be used in new code:

- `var` declarations (use `const` or `let`)
- Callback-based async (use async/await)
- `any` types (use specific types)
- `== ` or `!=` (use `===` or `!==`)
- `require()` (use ES6 imports)
- Global variables
- `eval()` or dynamic code execution

## Tool Configuration

### ESLint
All projects should have `.eslintrc.json` with:
- `"strict": true`
- `"prettier": true` for formatting integration
- No `// eslint-disable` without justification

### TypeScript
All projects should have `tsconfig.json` with:
- `"strict": true`
- `"noImplicitAny": true`
- `"strictNullChecks": true`
- Target ES2020 or later

### Prettier
All projects should have `.prettierrc` with:
- `"printWidth": 100`
- `"semi": true`
- `"singleQuote": true`
- `"trailingComma": "es5"`

## Documentation

### README Requirements
Every service/module needs a README.md with:
- Purpose and overview
- Architecture/design decisions
- Setup instructions
- Testing instructions
- Deployment instructions
- Known issues

### API Documentation
- Use OpenAPI/Swagger for REST APIs
- Generate documentation from code comments
- Keep docs in sync with implementation
- Include example requests and responses
